{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; git diff HEAD --stat; git diff HEAD -- docs/design/dao.jing.md test/dao/space/index_test.cljc test/dao/space/query_test.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; git diff HEAD --stat; git diff HEAD -- docs/design/dao.jing.md test/dao/space/index_test.cljc test/dao/space/query_test.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n docs/design/dao.jing.md             | 106 ++++++-----\n src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc              | 217 +++++++++++++---------\n src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc          |  16 +-\n src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc       |  14 +-\n src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc  |  14 +-\n test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc         |  31 ++--\n test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc         |  81 +++++----\n test/dao/jing_test.cljc             | 349 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------------\n test/dao/space/index_test.cljc      | 131 +++++++++-----\n test/dao/space/query_test.cljc      |  22 ++-\n test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc     |  31 ++--\n test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj   |  32 ++--\n test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc |  89 ++++++---\n 13 files changed, 734 insertions(+), 399 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.jing.md b/docs/design/dao.jing.md\nindex f98dd7d..f9658e9 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.jing.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.jing.md\n@@ -4,8 +4,8 @@ Status: implemented. The observer (`observer-state` / `observe-step!`) and the\n plain-data content-store handles described here are the current\n `src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code. What remains open — the final canonical\n encoding, durable observer checkpoints, explicit materialization\n-acknowledgement, garbage collection, and async hydration — is listed under\n-*Open items and current limitations*.\n+acknowledgement, the content write path as an effect stream, garbage collection,\n+and async hydration — is listed under *Open items and current limitations*.\n \n **Related documents:**\n \n@@ -92,8 +92,10 @@ DaoJing must distinguish pool members operationally long enough to maintain a\n cursor for each one. That association is observer state only. It is not\n written into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic\n meaning. In the implemented observer each member entry is plain data —\n-`{:stream <ref>, :cursor {:position n}, :status s}` — an operational record,\n-nothing more.\n+`{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>, :status s}` — an operational\n+record, nothing more. `observer-state` rejects a member that lacks a cursor\n+or a v2 reader before any operation. Besides successful observation, the only\n+way a member's cursor changes is through `adopt-cursor`.\n \n If a storage backend partitions content into physical buckets or shards, that\n placement is a backend concern, normally derived from the content hash. It is\n@@ -159,18 +161,24 @@ different pool streams is also a no-op after the first insertion. Consequently:\n \n The implemented write is `dao.jing/materialize!`: it derives the address from\n the payload alone (`segment-key`) and asks the backend's `:put-content-fn`\n-for an explicit verdict. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n+for an explicit verdict. A backend validates before it writes: the address must\n+be a segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n+nothing. `:inserted` means the value is durably stored now;\n `:present` means an equal value is already stored there, in which case the\n stored value is read back and verified. A collision in which an existing\n address contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n-overwrite, and throws loudly.\n+overwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n+`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n \n ### Canonical encoding\n \n Content addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n-same bytes on every participating platform. The canonical encoder is therefore\n-part of the storage contract even though the meaning of the encoded value is\n-not.\n+same bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n+distinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n+type (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n+name never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n+The canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n+meaning of the encoded value is not.\n \n Canonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n sets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n@@ -237,38 +245,45 @@ ever treated as a mutable root.\n \n ## Cursor tracking and recovery\n \n-An observer retains one cursor per active pool member and repeatedly calls\n-`ds/next`:\n-\n-- `{:ok payload, :cursor next-cursor}`: materialize the payload and advance\n-  that stream's cursor;\n-- `:blocked`: no payload is currently available from that stream;\n-- `:end`: that stream is closed; and\n-- `:daostream/gap`: the cursor is behind the retention boundary and that\n-  stream requires resynchronization.\n-\n-The implemented observer is `dao.jing/observer-state` (build the immutable\n-state for an explicit pool, one entry per member) and\n-`dao.jing/observe-step!` (poll the pool round-robin and process at most one\n-payload; on success it materializes the payload before advancing the member\n-cursor). The `:next` scheduling index keeps a continuously ready member from\n+The observation step is `dao.stream.observe`, shared with `forward` and\n+the VM. `dao.jing/observe-step!` polls the pool round-robin and processes at\n+most one payload per pool walk.\n+\n+The `dao.stream.observe/step` core runs the materialization effect before\n+advancing the cursor. If materialization throws, the exception propagates\n+before the cursor is advanced, so the caller's state is untouched and the\n+same payload is reprocessed from the same cursor once the backend succeeds.\n+\n+The pool's signals and cursor disciplines are:\n+\n+| signal                                                    | when                                                       | extra keys                                      | member cursor            |\n+| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n+| `:dao.stream/ok`                                          | a payload was materialized                                 | `:address`                                      | successor, from the step |\n+| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                     | empty pool, or every non-ended member blocked              | —                                               | unchanged                |\n+| `:dao.stream/end`                                         | every member has ended                                     | —                                               | unchanged                |\n+| `:dao.stream/gap`                                         | member `i`'s position was evicted                          | `:member i`, `:cursor` (the step's `:recovery`) | unchanged                |\n+| `/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed, or answered outside the contract | `:member i`, `:result` (the step's `:read`)     | unchanged                |\n+\n+An unrecognized transport answer is reported as a defect signal under\n+`:dao.stream/transport-error`, carrying the classified read under `:result`\n+with the raw answer retained inside it, and no action is taken\n+beyond declining to advance. Recovery is the caller's decision, made by\n+`adopt-cursor` with the reported `:cursor`.\n+\n+The `:next` scheduling index keeps a continuously ready member from\n starving another. Scheduling does not affect the resulting content set,\n because materialization is content-addressed, commutative, and idempotent.\n \n-A durable checkpoint records operational progress — a cursor per pool entry —\n-separately from the content-addressed KV data. Source identity may be needed\n-by a checkpoint mechanism to resume the correct transport, but it must not\n-enter the materialized content identity. Persisting such checkpoints and\n-running the observer loop over time are open items (see below).\n-\n ## Resource lifecycle\n \n The observer itself is a value: `observe-step!` owns no resources and has\n nothing to close — streams and storage handles retain responsibility for\n releasing their transport, file, database, or network resources.\n `dao.jing/close!` delegates to a handle's optional `:close-fn`; a handle\n-without one has nothing to release. A long-running runner that drives the pool\n-loop, and its resource policy, are open items (see below).\n+without one has nothing to release. A backend's close is idempotent; after\n+close, every entry point throws, and stored content is neither cleared nor\n+rewritten. A long-running runner that drives the pool loop, and its resource\n+policy, are open items (see below).\n \n ## Implemented surface\n \n@@ -294,19 +309,16 @@ Implemented backends:\n   content-addressed in-memory store. Put is an atomic insert-if-absent; an\n   address already holding the same payload reports `:present` and is never\n   overwritten.\n-- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` — a content-addressed store backed by an\n-  append-only log stream. Each log record is `[address payload]`, written\n+- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` — a content-addressed store backed by a\n+  private framed append-only file. Each log record is `[address payload]`, written\n   through a write lock, acknowledged only after the log is flushed, and\n   replayed on open to rebuild the in-memory content map. The framing layer\n   truncates an incomplete tail before replay; Jing then fails closed on any\n   complete record that cannot be decoded, validated, or matched to its content\n-  address.\n-- `dao.jing.remote` — exposes a local content handle as the `:jing/put-content`\n-  and `:jing/get-content` RPC ops (`default-handlers`) and wraps an RPC client\n-  as a content handle (`content-client`); `connect-content!` is the\n-  synchronous WebSocket constructor, JVM-only. `:jing/get-content` returns the\n-  exact wire envelope `{:found? boolean, :value value}`; caller-local\n-  not-found sentinels never cross the RPC boundary.\n+  address. The store guarantees idempotent close, throws after close, and\n+  serializes concurrent puts with exactly one record written.\n+- `dao.jing.remote` — currently on v1, awaiting `dao.space`'s plan to\n+  implement the stepped client design that replaces its synchronous handles.\n - `dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht` and\n   `dao.jing.dht.node/create-content-dht-udp` — the distributed backend over an\n   `IDhtNet` transport; see `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md`.\n@@ -330,13 +342,19 @@ encoder is transitional until the pinned canonical byte encoding lands.\n   exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   and every minted address change together.\n - **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n-  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint format\n-  for resuming pool cursors across restarts, and a runner that drives the\n-  loop over time, remain to be built.\n+  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n+  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n+  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n+  the contract). A runner that drives the loop over time remains to be built.\n - **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n+- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\n+  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\n+  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\n+  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\n+  write-path redesign.\n - **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   forever.\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc b/src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc\nindex 8c16301..b37d61f 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc\n+++ b/src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc\n@@ -1,14 +1,15 @@\n (ns dao.space.transactor\n   \"The `:transactor` stream: a single-writer wrapper over an explicit local\n-   `dao.stream` plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\n-   Publication from an agent).\n+   `dao.stream` plus an explicit intake pool of `dao.stream` writers\n+   (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Publication from an agent).\n \n    Descriptor:\n \n      {:dao.stream/type :transactor\n       :local-stream s   ; must satisfy IDaoStreamReader and IDaoStreamWriter;\n                         ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n-      :intake-pool [p]  ; non-empty; every member satisfies IDaoStreamWriter;\n+      :intake-pool [p]  ; non-empty; every member satisfies the dao.stream\n+                        ; writer surface (stream/writer?);\n                         ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n       :name n}          ; optional, diagnostic only\n \n@@ -40,7 +41,8 @@\n    neither closes nor erases the local stream.\"\n   (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n             [dao.space.index :as index]\n-            [dao.stream :as ds])\n+            [dao.stream :as ds]\n+            [dao.stream :as stream])\n   #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\n \n \n@@ -192,10 +194,10 @@\n     (when-not (and (coll? intake-pool) (seq intake-pool))\n       (throw\n         (ex-info\n-          \":tra